## Why Water Expands When It Freezes

### An Unusual Property Among Substances
Most liquids contract and become denser when they solidify because their molecules slow down and pack together more tightly. Water behaves in the opposite way: it expands by roughly 9% in volume when it freezes, making ice less dense than the liquid from which it formed. [1](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [7](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [9](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH)

### Molecular Structure and Hydrogen Bonds
A water molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom in a bent, polar arrangement. The oxygen atom draws electron density toward itself, giving that side a slight negative charge while the hydrogen side carries a slight positive charge. [4](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) This polarity causes neighboring water molecules to attract one another through hydrogen bonds. [4](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [7](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH)

### The Open Lattice of Ice
In liquid water, molecules are constantly moving and tumbling past one another; hydrogen bonds form and break millions of times per second, allowing the molecules to stay relatively close together. [7](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) Upon freezing, the molecules lose thermal energy and the hydrogen bonds lock into a rigid, permanent crystalline network. [1](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [7](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) Each molecule becomes bonded to four neighbors in a tetrahedral geometry with angles of about 109 degrees, forcing the molecules into an open hexagonal lattice. [2](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [7](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) This structured arrangement contains more empty space than the disordered liquid state, so the solid occupies a larger volume. [2](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [4](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH)

### Density, Floating Ice, and the 4 °C Anomaly
Because of this expansion, ice has a lower density than liquid water, which is why ice floats with approximately 8% of its mass above the surface. [2](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) Water also reaches its maximum density at about 4 °C; as it cools below that temperature, it begins to expand slightly as hydrogen-bonded clusters that prefigure the ice lattice become more prevalent. [2](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH)

### Real-World Consequences
The fact that ice floats causes lakes and oceans to freeze from the top down, creating an insulating layer that helps protect aquatic life below. [2](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [9](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) The same expansion generates tremendous pressure that can crack rocks, rupture pavement, and burst water pipes in freezing temperatures. [1](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH) [7](#d2stEuHCHWEcwODH)

### Conclusion
In short, water expands upon freezing because its polar molecules lock into an open, hexagonal crystalline lattice via hydrogen bonds, creating more empty space than in the constantly shifting liquid state.

